\section{Planetary Simulation Architecture}
\label{sec:planetary_architecture}

This section describes the integrated planetary-class simulation
architecture used to model Saturn as a multi-physics, multi-layer
computational vessel. The architecture couples collisional ring dynamics,
gravitational potential solvers, magnetohydrodynamic plasma flow, and
atmospheric radiative transfer within a unified execution framework.

\subsection{Subsystem Decomposition}
The planetary engine is organized into four primary subsystems:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Ring N-body Collisional Solver
    \item Gravitational Potential \& Shape Solver
    \item Magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) Plasma \& Magnetosphere
    \item Atmospheric Radiative Transfer \& Shader Pipeline
\end{itemize}

Each subsystem is executed under a supervisor kernel (SDSE-77) that
coordinates stress testing, synchronization, and telemetry logging.

\subsection{Ring N-Body Collisional Solver}
The ring subsystem evolves a high-density particle ensemble:
\[
N_{\text{ring}} \approx 1.2 \times 10^9,
\]
using a spatial hashing grid and collisional statistics derived from
mean free path and collision frequency:
\[
\ell \approx 1.82 \; \text{m}, \qquad
\gamma_c \approx 4.12 \times 10^4 \; \text{collisions} \; \text{s}^{-1} \text{m}^{-3}.
\]

Dynamic restitution decay is applied to model energy dissipation in
dense ring regions, with performance maintained at:
\[
\ge 60 \; \text{FPS}
\]
on the GPU compute pipeline.

\subsection{Gravitational Potential \& Shape Solver}
The planetary gravity field is represented using zonal harmonics:
\[
J_2, J_4, J_6, J_8,
\]
and integrated with a symplectic leapfrog scheme:
\[
\Delta F / F < 10^{-7}
\]
under stress conditions. The solver tracks Saturn and its satellite
ensemble:
\[
N_{\text{bodies}} = 1 + 146,
\]
ensuring consistent orbital dynamics and shape-dependent potential.

\subsection{Magnetosphere \& MHD Plasma}
The magnetospheric subsystem models:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Enceladus plasma torus ion density,
    \item field-aligned current loops,
    \item bow shock and magnetopause position.
\end{itemize}

Solar wind pressure spikes up to:
\[
5.5 \times \text{nominal},
\]
are injected to simulate CME-like compression events, driving the
magnetopause inward from:
\[
22 R_s \rightarrow 16.5 R_s,
\]
while maintaining grid stability and auroral current coherence.

\subsection{Atmospheric Radiative Transfer \& Shader Pipeline}
The atmospheric subsystem couples:
\begin{itemize}
    \item radiative transfer through haze and cloud layers,
    \item Mie and Rayleigh scattering,
    \item high-velocity shear winds (up to 750 \; \text{m/s}),
\end{itemize}
on an octahedral fluid grid. The shader pipeline maps physical state
variables (density, temperature, composition) to visual band dynamics
without inducing numerical blow-up.

\subsection{Supervisor Kernel \& Stress Testing}
All subsystems are orchestrated by the SDSE-77 supervisor kernel in
\texttt{STRESS\_TEST\_MODE: FULL\_SYNCHRONIZATION}. Real-time telemetry
tracks:
\begin{itemize}
    \item GPU memory allocation and frame latency,
    \item active particle and body counts,
    \item plasma and auroral flux metrics.
\end{itemize}

Stress-induced phenomena such as self-gravity wakes, viscous
overstability, and magnetospheric compression are used as qualitative
and quantitative validation signatures of correct planetary behavior.

